// Claude-Template -- product-owner workspace (role `po`).
//
// ONE COLOUR KEY, NOT FOUR. THE REGISTRY DECIDES, PEACOCK APPLIES.
// This file carried no window colour at all until now -- and it deliberately
// does NOT get one through the four `workbench.colorCustomizations` keys its
// siblings used to carry. One key is the only arrangement that WORKS while
// Peacock is installed. `johnpapa.vscode-peacock` activates on
// `"activationEvents": ["*"]`, i.e. in EVERY window, owns titleBar/statusBar/
// activityBar by default, and on `onDidChangeConfiguration` runs, in substance:
//
//     const t = getEnvironmentAwareColor();          // `peacock.color` -> "" when unset
//     const o = getCurrentColorBeforeAdjustments();  // the EXISTING customizations
//     if (peacockSettingsChanged && (t || o)) { await applyColor(t); ... }
//
// Four `workbench.colorCustomizations` keys here would make `o` truthy while
// `t` stayed empty, so Peacock would apply "no colour" -- and DELETE them again.
// The symptom is a colour that flashes up once and vanishes. Two owners for one
// fact, and the other owner wins every time.
//
// One owner now: the registry decides WHICH colour, `peacock.color` hands it
// over, Peacock applies it. Without the extension nothing reads that key and the
// window stays grey -- the accepted price for not having two writers on one
// surface, and the reason Peacock is a declared prerequisite.
//
// THIS BELONGS IN A `*.code-workspace`, NOT IN `.vscode/settings.json`, because
// this file is OPT-IN: it binds whoever opens THIS file, and nothing else. A
// committed folder setting would colour every teammate's window on clone, and
// opting out would mean editing a versioned file.
//
// SEE: `claude-skills/claude-skills.code-workspace` -- the canonical file, with
//      every measurement written out -- plus the `color-registry` skill and
//      ADR-0043 (Nachtrag 2026-08-03).
{
  "folders": [
    { "name": "📋 Scrum Board", "path": ".scrum" },
    { "name": "📄 Dokumentation", "path": "docs" },
    { "name": "🏠 Projekt Root", "path": "." }
  ],
  "settings": {
    // Generated -- do not hand-edit. Regenerate with:
    //   npx tsx -e "import {windowColors} from 'C:/Entwicklung/claude-skills/src/colorRegistry.ts'; console.log(JSON.stringify(windowColors('Claude-Template','po')))"
    // color-registry: project=Claude-Template (slot 2, hue 60) x role=po (rung 7, L 33%)
    // oklch(33% 0.0774 60) -> #512a00 -- contrast against #ffffff = 12.49:1
    //
    // HEX ONLY. Peacock validates with tinycolor and VS Code parses colour
    // customizations with `fromHex(i){ return parseHex(i) || red }` -- an
    // `oklch(...)`, `hsl(...)` or colour name ends up PURE RED with no error, no
    // log line and no theme fallback. oklch is the authoring space, hex is the
    // delivery format.
    //
    // THE ONE COLOUR FACT. Peacock derives every surface it is allowed to touch
    // from this single value: titleBar (`affectTitleBar`, default true) and
    // statusBar (`affectStatusBar`, default true). The activity bar would be the
    // third -- it is switched OFF at the end of this block, and that is measured,
    // not taste.
    "peacock.color": "#512a00",

    // The registry's own foreground, handed to Peacock. Peacock picks one with
    // `isLight(bg) ? darkForegroundColor : lightForegroundColor`, and the whole
    // palette is dark by construction (highest YIQ brightness 110.3), so the
    // LIGHT candidate is what 99 of 99 windows get -- the dark one is never
    // reached and is therefore not the problem. Peacock's own default #e7e7e7
    // puts 14 of those 99 below 4.5:1 (minimum 3.86, at global-login/project);
    // #ffffff puts the minimum at 4.77 and none below.
    "peacock.lightForegroundColor": "#ffffff",

    // A ratchet, not a no-op, even though it equals the default: workspace beats
    // user, so this line is what stops a `true` in somebody's USER settings from
    // voiding the one above. `true` is the DANGEROUS value here, not the safe
    // one -- in `prepareColors` the foreground keys are written only under
    // `if (!keepForegroundColor)`, so Peacock would write BACKGROUNDS ONLY and
    // leave the foreground to the theme. Against this VS Code's own default
    // themes that is 36 of 99 failing (Dark Modern #cccccc, min 2.97) up to
    // 99 of 99 (Dark 2026 #8c8c8c, Light Modern #3b3b3b, Light 2026 #606060) --
    // i.e. a theme switch would silently take the colour contract with it.
    "peacock.keepForegroundColor": false,

    // WE DO NOT COLOUR A SURFACE WHOSE CONTRAST WE CANNOT PROMISE -- decided
    // 2026-08-03. Peacock lightens the activity bar via
    // `elementAdjustments.activityBar: "lighten"` (its own default), BY 10 AND
    // NOT BY 5: `getAdjustedColorHex` calls the lighten helper WITHOUT an amount,
    // so it falls back to `defaultAmountToDarkenLighten = 10`, while
    // `peacock.darkenLightenPercentage` (default 5) is read ONLY by the manual
    // Darken/Lighten commands and never touches this path. Measured with +10 HSL
    // lightness that surface runs 2.62 .. 10.91 with 30 of 99 combinations below
    // 4.5:1. Coloured and covered instead: titleBar + statusBar, 4.77 .. 14.44
    // over all 99 combinations, none below 4.5:1.
    "peacock.affectActivityBar": false,

    // ---- KEPT, AND NOT PART OF THE COLOUR PATTERN ------------------------
    // Pre-existing project configuration below this line. It carries no colour
    // and touches no surface Peacock owns, so it was left exactly as it was --
    // for the same reason `folders` and `extensions` were left alone. This
    // change owns the window colour and nothing else.
    "workbench.iconTheme": "material-icon-theme",
    "workbench.productIconTheme": "material-product-icons"
  },
  "extensions": {
    "recommendations": [
      "pablodelucca.pixel-agents",
      "PKief.material-icon-theme",
      "PKief.material-product-icons",
      "yzhang.markdown-all-in-one",
      "DavidAnson.vscode-markdownlint",
      "Gruntfuggly.todo-tree",
      "dotenv.dotenv-vscode"
    ]
  }
}
